# bold5x7 - the same body text, heavy enough to survive the glass.
#
# Two-way mirror film passes 10 to 30 percent of the light behind it, and what
# it takes first is the thinnest strokes. A 1px stem at 20 percent transmission
# is the thing that disappears while everything around it stays readable, which
# is why this exists: every stem is 2px, so dimming loses brightness rather
# than losing the letter.
#
# Same 7-row cell and baseline as tom5x7, so it is a drop-in swap on any widget
# and the vertical rhythm of a layout does not move. It is wider: caps are 6px
# against 5px, which costs about 30 percent of the line. "Standup 10:00 Design"
# measures 94px in tom5x7 and 123px here, so a 64px column fits noticeably less.
# That is the trade. For a headline or a temperature it is usually worth it;
# keep tom5x7 for dense agenda rows.
#
# Vertical metrics in the 7-row cell:
#   rows 0-5   capitals, digits, ascenders
#   rows 1-5   lowercase x-height
#   row  6     descenders (g j p q y) and the comma tail
#
# Codepoint 127 carries the degree sign, exactly as in tom5x7, so "21<127>C"
# renders in either font without the layout changing.

@name     bold5x7
@role     text
@height   7
@baseline 6
@gap      1

# --- space and punctuation ---
